    "summary": "SNMP bruteforce leaks API creds, command injection in an admin endpoint drops a docker shell, and API model tampering plus password reuse give root.",
    "md": "# Mentor - HackTheBox - Medium\n\n---\n\n## Reconnaissance\n\nFirst thing I do is see if we have connection with the machine.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/01.png)\n\nFrom here I do the same two scans to see which ports and services are open.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/02.png)\n![](img/htb-mentor/03.png)\n\nI also did a UDP scan to see what UDP services we have running. From now on I will do this with every machine, because enumerating is one of the most important things to do on a machine.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/04.png)\n![](img/htb-mentor/05.png)\n\nBefore going on the website I used whatweb to see which technologies it's using.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/06.png)\n\nFrom here we can see it's doing virtual hosting so we have to add the hostname, to see what's on the website. Now we relaunch whatweb and check all the technologies. We can see we have python, and one named Werkzeug.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/07.png)\n![](img/htb-mentor/08.png)\n\n---\n\n## Enumeration\n\nFrom here we can see port 22, 80, and 161. So from here I started enumerating port 80 while I was also enumerating port 161.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/09.png)\n![](img/htb-mentor/10.png)\n\nFrom here I didn't get anything interesting so I tried to enumerate for virtual hosts too.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/11.png)\n\nWe can see an API subdomain, so we are going to try to enumerate it. But first we have to add this subdomain to the /etc/hosts file.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/12.png)\n\nAnd we can see the documentation of the API. And we can see a possible user named james.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/13.png)\n![](img/htb-mentor/14.png)\n![](img/htb-mentor/15.png)\n\n---\n\n## Foothold: OS command injection via the admin backup endpoint\n\nFrom here I tried to create a user an log in.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/16.png)\n\nNow that we have created the account we can try and login with this credentials. We can log in so we successfully created an account with the API. Now we can see we get a token by the structure we can see it's a JWT.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/17.png)\n\nI tried to access the admin directory but we can't because only admin user can.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/18.png)\n\nFrom here I didn't know where to continue, so I went to check the writeup, I saw we can bruteforce the snmp service to get any other community strings. This is the [tool](https://github.com/SECFORCE/SNMP-Brute) I used.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/19.png)\n\nWe found an internal community string, so we are going to try and check what's on it. I got some login with a possible credential.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/20.png)\n![](img/htb-mentor/21.png)\n\nFrom here we got some possible credentials for the username we found named james.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/22.png)\n\nAnd we can log in, we can try to enumerate more the admin endpoint and others. On the admin endpoint I found a backup folder.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/23.png)\n\nWe can try to access with the session we created. To use this endpoint we have to use a POST request.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/24.png)\n![](img/htb-mentor/25.png)\n\nAfter making the POST petition we see we need a field required. This field is path.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/26.png)\n\nFrom here I didn't know what to do with this, so I looked up for a hint, I saw we can do a command injection on this field. To try if it works I will try to get connection from the machine. And as we can see we have connection from the box to our machine, and we also can confirm the OS command injection.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/27.png)\n\nFrom here we can get a shell, the only shell that worked for me was a shell with netcat. When I'm in, I do a whoami command and I see root straight away. This is kind of strange, because getting root immediately is not what I'd expect from this kind of RCE, so I suspect I'm not on the real machine but inside some kind of container. Either way, from here I can get the user.txt flag.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/28.png)\n![](img/htb-mentor/29.png)\n\n---\n\n## Privilege escalation: credential leak via API model tampering, password reuse, and a sudo misconfiguration\n\nWhen we do ip a we can see that we are on another network. And on the root directory I found a dockerenv, so it means we are inside of a docker.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/30.png)\n![](img/htb-mentor/31.png)\n\nFrom here I configured ligolo-ng so I can use my tools on the intranetwork. Or that was the plan but I ended up not doing this, instead I started looking around because I was having a lot of trouble to get ligolo-ng-agent to work on the docker machine. I also found we are inside of alpine. I found a possible way to connect to a database in one of the files on the app directory.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/32.png)\n![](img/htb-mentor/33.png)\n\nBut we don't have psql installed, so we can or either fighting with the os to get the chisel or ligolo running or do this trick from IppSec. So the trick consists of the users.py, on the api directory from the app we can see that when we list all the users, we do a GET petition to the database using a model. So what happens if we change the models on this directory and add one that returns the password, will the database return this password on the model?\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/34.png)\n![](img/htb-mentor/35.png)\n\nAfter changing this we just have to do the petition with the API to get the passwords from all the users registered.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/36.png)\n\nWe can see it might be stored as MD5, so we have to go and crack it using CrackStation. And we get a password for the user service_acc. The user isn't called service_acc, I actually found it's svc, this is because it's the home directory we had on the machine.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/37.png)\n\nNow we can try and log in with this credentials on the ssh server.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/38.png)\n\nRunning linpeas as this user I found a password. On the snmpd.config, I tried this password on the users that we have on the /etc/passwd.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/39.png)\n![](img/htb-mentor/40.png)\n\nAnd james worked.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/41.png)\n\nFrom here I started again trying to escalate privileges. I saw we can execute /bin/sh with sudo, and it only asks for James's own password, not root's password. And we are root on the machine not the docker, so we can now get the flag, and finish the machine.\n\n![](img/htb-mentor/42.png)\n![](img/htb-mentor/43.png)\n![](img/htb-mentor/44.png)\n![](img/htb-mentor/45.png)\n\n---\n\n## Reflection\n\nGetting a shell with root user doesn't mean you got everything you can get.\nIt's not necessary to use tools if you can just use the application to get the resources you want.\nAPI enumeration is important.\nUDP enumeration is important.\n"
