    "summary": "Plaintext creds in SNMP give SSH access, a port-forwarded vhost exposes a vulnerable Pandora FMS, and SQLi-based RCE plus a SUID binary's PATH hijack give root.",
    "md": "# Pandora - Hack The Box (Medium)\n\n---\n\n## Reconnaissance\n\nFirst thing I'm going to do is check that the machine is up.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/01.png)\n\nThen from here I did the two scans to see which ports and services we have on the machine.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/02.png)\n![](img/htb-pandora/03.png)\n\nFrom here we can see that we have port 80, so I used whatweb to see any hostname and technologies that can be used. We can see that the server uses Apache 2.4.41, and we can see a possible domain named `panda.htb`.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/04.png)\n![](img/htb-pandora/05.png)\n\n---\n\n## Enumeration\n\nWe can see a WordPress, so the first thing I did was enumerate this WordPress with `wpscan`, but the scan didn't work. Maybe it's a false positive, as in the whatweb tool we could see that it might be running WordPress, but it was not 100% sure.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/06.png)\n\nSo next I started scanning for possible directories and files with some common extensions.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/07.png)\n\nWhile this was running, I started enumerating, as on the machine info I could see that we had SNMP, so I launched an nmap for the UDP protocol. As we can see we have an SNMP service, so while the gobuster is running I'm going to enumerate this.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/08.png)\n\nAfter running `snmpwalk`, I found this username and password in plain text, because it's a program executing on the terminal.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/09.png)\n![](img/htb-pandora/10.png)\n\nNow that we have a user, we can try and reuse these credentials on the SSH service. And it worked, so we no longer need to search the website, as it might be a rabbit hole.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/11.png)\n\nWe can see that we can't open `user.txt` in the `matt` directory, so we might have to move laterally to get this flag.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/12.png)\n\n---\n\n## Lateral Movement\n\nOn the web directory I found some SQL files, so I started looking around to see what I can find, but I didn't find anything.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/13.png)\n\nSo I kept looking around, but I didn't find anything, so I looked at the writeup, and found a `sites-enabled` folder that contains a virtual host configuration for the Pandora site.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/14.png)\n\nSo I did a local port forward with SSH, and now when we load our port 8080 on localhost we can see what's on the virtual host on port 80 of that machine.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/15.png)\n![](img/htb-pandora/16.png)\n\nWe can see a version of Pandora FMS.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/17.png)\n\nSearching for vulnerabilities I found a lot of RCEs, but most of them needed an admin account. But after some time I found a SQLi vulnerability. [GitHub](https://github.com/shyam0904a/Pandora_v7.0NG.742_exploit_unauthenticated)\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/18.png)\n\nSo I downloaded it and used it. We could have done this with sqlmap or manually, but it would have taken a lot of time.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/19.png)\n\nFrom here we can send a reverse shell to our machine to have a better shell: first we check we can reach our host, and then do the reverse shell.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/20.png)\n\nSo I changed the payload, and instead of launching a web shell I executed a reverse shell.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/21.png)\n\nNow when we execute we'll get the same as before, but now when we run a command it will send a reverse shell, it doesn't matter what command.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/22.png)\n\nFrom here we can get the `user.txt` flag.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/23.png)\n\n---\n\n## Privilege escalation: PATH hijacking via the pandora_backup SUID binary\n\nWe can see a `pandora_backup` script that has SUID permissions.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/24.png)\n\nSo I downloaded this file to my machine to see what was in this binary.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/25.png)\n\nWe can see that it executes `tar` without a full path, so if we can modify our `PATH` we can do a PATH hijack.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/26.png)\n\nNow that we have our `PATH` hijacked, we can create a file called `tar` in the `/tmp` directory and add malicious code. In this case it will copy `/bin/bash` and add SUID permissions.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/27.png)\n\nSo what should happen is: when we execute the binary, since `/tmp` is the first directory in `PATH`, when it looks for the `tar` executable it will find our malicious `tar` file, and it will copy the bash binary, because root is the one executing this file.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/28.png)\n\nBut it didn't work, so from here I started reading a writeup and found that we need to actually have a persistent session, because the shell we get from the RCE exploit doesn't give us one: each command runs as its own one-off process, so the `export PATH=...` from one command doesn't carry over to the next. So we have to create an SSH key, log in as `matt`, and then we could try to run it again, this time from a real interactive shell where the exported `PATH` actually sticks.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/29.png)\n![](img/htb-pandora/30.png)\n![](img/htb-pandora/31.png)\n\nNow we're going to try again, and now we do have a root shell. The thing I wasn't doing right was not having a persistent session.\n\n![](img/htb-pandora/32.png)\n![](img/htb-pandora/33.png)\n![](img/htb-pandora/34.png)\n\n---\n\n## Reflection\n\nWith this machine I learned or reviewed these concepts:\n\n1. Nmap UDP scan\n2. SSH port forwarding\n3. PATH hijacking\n4. Creating an SSH key to actually generate a persistent session\n5. RCE doesn't always generate a persistent session\n"
