    "summary": "A UNION-based SQLi in a gaming-tournament signup writes a PHP webshell via INTO OUTFILE, then a reused database password gives root through su.",
    "md": "# Validation - Hack The Box (Easy)\n\n---\n\n## Reconnaissance\n\nThe first thing I did was check that the machine is up.\n\n![](img/htb-validation/01.png)\n\nFrom here I launched the two scans to see which ports and services we had available.\n\n![](img/htb-validation/02.png)\n![](img/htb-validation/03.png)\n\nWe can see a web service, so let's check the technologies first with `whatweb`.\n\n![](img/htb-validation/04.png)\n\nFuzzing for directories, I found a `config.php`, but I couldn't download it.\n\n![](img/htb-validation/05.png)\n\n---\n\n## A cookie that's just MD5(username)\n\nI went into the website and intercepted the username request.\n\n![](img/htb-validation/06.png)\n\nWe can see the cookie it generates is MD5, and it's related to the username we submitted.\n\n![](img/htb-validation/07.png)\n![](img/htb-validation/08.png)\n\n---\n\n## SQL injection via the country field\n\nSo what we try is a SQLi on the field that looks less obvious, the `country` one. Then we go to `account.php` with this cookie, and we can see an error coming straight from the database.\n\n![](img/htb-validation/09.png)\n\nThis works because the `country` value we submit at login gets stored and reused later, unsanitized, in a query run by `account.php` (that's why the page literally echoes back what we sent, and why the syntax error only shows up there, on the next request, not on the login page itself).\n\nSo now we can try a UNION SELECT, to check it works and we no longer get errors.\n\n![](img/htb-validation/10.png)\n![](img/htb-validation/11.png)\n\nAfter enumerating, we found we can only display data through one column of the UNION, so we use `CONCAT` to pack several values into it. From here we can enumerate the existing databases.\n\n![](img/htb-validation/12.png)\n\nFrom here I enumerated the tables in the `mysql` database.\n\n![](img/htb-validation/13.png)\n![](img/htb-validation/14.png)\n\n---\n\n## RCE via UNION SELECT ... INTO OUTFILE\n\nBut from here I got nothing of value, just users. So, looking at a public writeup, I saw that with `UNION` you can write the selected text straight to a file on disk: if we select a small PHP webshell, MySQL will write it for us, and we get a shell. This is how I uploaded it (see the full payload in the request below):\n\n![](img/htb-validation/15.png)\n![](img/htb-validation/16.png)\n\nIt abuses the fact that the UNION's result can be written directly into the web root with `INTO OUTFILE`, since the database user has the `FILE` privilege and the directory is writable.\n\n---\n\n## Getting a shell\n\nFrom here I launched a reverse shell. The payload is a simple base64-encoded reverse shell: the command decodes it with `base64 -d` and then executes the output. It's important to URL-encode it.\n\n![](img/htb-validation/17.png)\n![](img/htb-validation/18.png)\n\nWith this user we can get `user.txt`.\n\n![](img/htb-validation/19.png)\n\n---\n\n## Privilege escalation: reused database password\n\nFrom `/var/www/html` I found the `config.php` I'd seen at the start, which has credentials for the database.\n\n![](img/htb-validation/20.png)\n\nThe password contains the word \"global\", so it might be reused. I tried logging in as `root` with this same password with `su`, and it worked.\n\n![](img/htb-validation/21.png)\n\nFrom here we got the root flag.\n\n![](img/htb-validation/22.png)\n![](img/htb-validation/23.png)"
