    "summary": "A chained SSRF bypasses a blacklist to reach an internal admin panel, leaking FTP creds and an SSH key, then a sudo script's pdb.post_mortem gives a root shell.",
    "md": "# Forge - Hack The Box (Medium)\n\n---\n\n## Reconnaissance\n\nThe first thing I did was seeing if the machine is up.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/01.png)\n\nFrom here I did the two scans that I normally do, to see which ports and services are up.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/02.png)\n![](img/htb-forge/03.png)\n\nWe can see port 80 open, so from here I will use whatweb to see which technologies we have running. We can see the hostname, so we might have to add it to the `/etc/hosts`, if I'm not wrong this is because it uses virtual hosting.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/04.png)\n\nNow we can see that we have Apache 2.4.41, and the title. So now I'm going to check the website on the browser.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/05.png)\n\nHere we can see a gallery, and a button that can let us upload images.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/06.png)\n\n---\n\n## Finding the SSRF in main.js\n\nBefore uploading an image I fuzzed to see which directories or files we have on the server.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/07.png)\n\nFrom here I looked into the `static` and `uploads` directories. I couldn't access the `uploads` directory, so I went to the `static` one.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/08.png)\n\nHere I found this `js` directory that contained the main JavaScript code for the web page.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/09.png)\n\nOn this code we can see a `show_upload_remote_file`, this is interesting, because we might be able to load things that we can not see but the server can. So I tried to extract the content from `/etc/passwd`. But we can see it only accepts http and https requests, so from here I'm thinking about an SSRF, so I will intercept this petition with Burp Suite.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/10.png)\n![](img/htb-forge/11.png)\n\n---\n\n## Bypassing the SSRF blacklist\n\nAfter trying to do a petition to localhost, we can see that we have a blacklist, so we have to try until we can make a petition to localhost.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/12.png)\n\nOn my notes I had ways to bypass this, and the one that worked was this one. This works because by just putting `127.1` we are already referring to localhost, so the machine resolves to the loopback that ends up being `127.0.0.1`.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/13.png)\n\nThis is what the petition responds to. We can see that it gives us an error. We can try to enumerate a subdomain to use it to make a petition that will come from the server.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/14.png)\n![](img/htb-forge/15.png)\n\nAnd we found something good, we have an admin subdomain. So I added it to the `/etc/hosts`.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/16.png)\n![](img/htb-forge/17.png)\n\nWe can see only localhost is allowed, so what happens now if we make the petition directly to this URL? We can see only localhost is allowed, so now we have to bypass the `admin.forge.htb` URL blacklist. Something we can try is add some capital letters to the hostname.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/18.png)\n\nAnd it worked. Let's check what we can see now.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/19.png)\n![](img/htb-forge/20.png)\n\n---\n## Leaking FTP credentials and getting user.txt\n\nWe can see an `/announcements` directory, so we are going to check that now. And boom, we can see some credentials for the FTP server.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/21.png)\n![](img/htb-forge/22.png)\n\nWe can see that we can connect via FTP to the server and to the upload endpoint, with the SSRF. So what I did was point to the `/upload` from the admin subdomain, and from there do the SSRF, with FTP, with the user and password.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/23.png)\n![](img/htb-forge/24.png)\n\nAnd from here we can get the `user.txt`.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/25.png)\n\n---\n\n## Getting a shell\n\nNow we need to get a shell. We can see that we have SSH service on, so we can try and get the private key to log in. From here I started using `curl`, because it's faster, I don't know why I didn't start doing this earlier.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/26.png)\n\nNow we are getting the private key, because we don't know if the public one will work, because we don't know if it's on the `authorized_keys`.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/27.png)\n\nNow we use this one to log in to the server. We are inside now.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/28.png)\n\n---\n\n## Privilege escalation: sudo script and pdb.post_mortem\n\nFirst thing I did was checking what we can execute with sudo, and saw a script that we are going to check.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/29.png)\n![](img/htb-forge/30.png)\n\nWe can see that this script opens a service on a random port. From here it asks us to enter a password, this password is in plain text on the same script, and if we enter this password correctly we can see it gives us a menu to use. These options will end up executing different commands on the machine as the user that executes the script, in this case root, and will return the output to us. Then we have an exception with `pdb.post_mortem`, I didn't know what this was so I searched online before launching the script. I remembered that `pdb` is a breakpoint, so we can see that this will open a breakpoint on the code whenever an error occurs, the thing is that it will give us this debugging point for the error.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/31.png)\n\nSo before starting I got another shell. And then used this script.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/32.png)\n\nThen, instead of a correct option, I used something incorrect, then we can see the pdb session as root.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/33.png)\n\nNow, if we are root on a pdb, we can execute Python.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/34.png)\n\nSo now we can just spawn a shell and we should be root. And it worked, now we can just get the root flag.\n\n![](img/htb-forge/35.png)\n![](img/htb-forge/36.png)\n![](img/htb-forge/37.png)\n"
